{{nihongo|''Okaiken Airways Co., Ltd.''|岡井空輸株式会社|Okai Kūyu Kabushiki-gaisha}}, operating under the brand name {{nihongo|''Okaiken Air''|岡井航空|Okai-Kōkū}} and stylized as ''OkaikenAir'', is the flagship carrier and primary airline of the ''Imperial Kingdom of Okaiken''. Headquartered within the high-density Chiyoda District of [[Okami]], it maintains a reputation as a global leader in aviation and is consistently ranked among the most elite carriers in the world. As of 2024, the airline operates an expansive network that includes sixty-two domestic destinations across the islands of [[Harujima]], [[Ishijima]], and [[Yagikoshu]], alongside forty-six international routes connecting the kingdom to the continents of [[Fosia]] and [[Illypnia]]. With primary international gateways located at ''Okami-Tobu'' and ''Shirakawa Tenno'', the airline functions as a vital conduit for the nation's technocratic and diplomatic missions. On 29 March 2013, the airline achieved a significant milestone by becoming the first in its region to be awarded a prestigious ''5-Star Airline'' rating, a distinction it has successfully defended for over a decade.

==History==
The origins of Okaiken Airways are rooted in the post-war reconstruction period following the surrender in 1952. While early aviation in the archipelago was fragmented among minor regional charter services, the formal establishment of the carrier on 1 August 1955 marked the beginning of a coordinated national aerospace strategy. Originally founded as a joint venture between the Ministry of Transport and several merchant guilds from Aizawa, the airline was designed to serve as the aerial counterpart to the expanding rail network. During this initial era, known as the Sanese Industrial Miracle, the government provided massive subsidies through the Okaiken Railways Group to ensure that the fledgling airline could acquire modern pressurized aircraft from Western Fosia. This allowed the carrier to establish the "Golden Triangle" route connecting Okami, Toyozawa, and Aizawa, effectively bypassing the rugged mountain ranges that made early ground travel between the islands treacherous.

By the early 1960s, the airline entered the jet age with the acquisition of the first P-120 short-haul jets, which drastically reduced travel times between Harujima and the southern industrial hubs of Yagikoshu. However, the stability of this period was shattered by the political upheaval of the [[Quiet Revolution]] in 1968. As the nation transitioned into a Federal Republic, the new socialist-leaning administration viewed the airline’s private-sector ties and its "Imperial" branding as remnants of a feudal past. Consequently, the government seized all private shares and merged the carrier with several regional post-war cooperatives to form the State Aviation Syndicate. Under nationalization, the airline’s focus shifted from profit to "Socialist Connectivity," where routes to sparsely populated sub-arctic prefectures like Kii and Aomori were prioritized over the lucrative international corridors to Fosia. While this era expanded flight access to the rural proletariat, it also led to a period of technical stagnation and aging equipment as the republic struggled with the inflationary crises of the 1970s.

The trajectory of the company changed fundamentally in 1984 during the "Great Privatization" led by the Republican government’s pivot toward a market economy. The airline was sold to the newly formed Okaiken Holdings Group, a massive conglomerate with ties to the technocratic elite. Re-establishing itself as Okaiken Airways, the company abandoned its austerity-era "Value Economy Class" service in favor of a strategic pivot toward the luxury market, competing directly with the international carriers of Trentannia and Shayden. This period saw the launch of the Diamond Service, the precursor to modern business class, and the adoption of the "Avatar" callsign, signifying the airline's role as the technological representative of the Sanese people. The subsequent economic bubble of the late 1980s fueled a massive expansion of the fleet, with the airline ordering dozens of high-capacity long-haul jets to service the burgeoning corporate class.

The most transformative era in the airline’s modern history arrived with the Jutsu Restoration of 2011. Following the return of the Monarchy and the rise of the technocratic state, the airline underwent a comprehensive ''rebranding'' ho o align itself with the new national identity. The Jutsu-era reforms integrated the Satori AI into the airline's flight operations and cabin management systems, transitioning the company from a traditional transport provider to a "flying laboratory" for Sanese innovation. The contemporary basalt-black and royal blue livery was introduced during this period to evoke the colors of the Imperial Navy’s Black Fleet, emphasizing the airline's role as a vital pillar of national sovereignty. This synergy between tradition and technology solidified the carrier’s position as the premier airline of the East Fosian Sphere, leading directly to its elevation to five-star status in 2013 and its current dominance of the Fosian air corridors.

==Corporate Affairs and Identity==
The company is widely recognized for its ''Hospitality of the High Ground'' philosophy, which is rooted in the Sanese concept of ''Masugu'', or straightforward integrity. Its branding features the ''Five Stars of Fosia'' emblem, representing its dominance over the major air corridors of the region. Through a strategic partnership with [[Kizan Systems]], the airline utilizes the ''Satori AI'' to optimize flight paths and fuel efficiency, a technocratic integration that has resulted in an average system-wide delay of less than 45 seconds.

==Five-Star Distinction==
The designation of Okaiken Air as a 5-Star airline is not merely a reflection of its modern equipment, but a result of a multi-layered commitment to what the company calls "Technocratic Hospitality." This rare status, maintained since 2013, is underpinned by several foundational programs that differentiate the Sanese flying experience from its international competitors. Central to this is the Omotenashi Protocol, a rigorous service framework that mandates cabin crew anticipate passenger needs before they are articulated. This philosophy is rooted in traditional Sanese social structures, where the host assumes total responsibility for the guest's comfort, yet it is executed with a precision and speed that mirrors the nation's industrial efficiency. 

Culinary excellence serves as another primary pillar of the airline's high ranking. Through the Taste of the Three Islands initiative, Okaiken Air has revolutionized in-flight dining by abandoning standardized airline catering in favor of a decentralized supply chain. Michelin-starred chefs from Okami, Mito, and Aizawa collaborate to design seasonal menus that change every sixty days to reflect the shifting micro-climates of the archipelago. In the Imperial First and Diamond Business cabins, meals are served "on-demand" rather than at fixed intervals, utilizing specialized induction heating technology that ensures the aggressive, piquant flavors of Sanese cuisine are preserved at high altitudes without the dehydration common in standard convection ovens.

The airline's technological infrastructure provides the third essential component of its 5-Star status. Okaiken Air operates the youngest long-haul fleet globally, consisting entirely of new aircraft equipped with advancements in Comfort and Technol, creating a "Smart Cabin" that is unique to the Sanese aviation sector. This technological edge extends to the Satori Sky-Sync system, which provides uninterrupted, high-bandwidth connectivity even in the sub-arctic latitudes of the southern routes.

Finally, the airline maintains a strict bilingual mandate that acts as a bridge between Okaiken's isolationist past and its globalized future. This ensures that staff are not just conversationally fluent, but technically proficient in both Trent and Sanese, allowing them to assist with complex diplomatic or technical inquiries mid-flight. This human element, combined with the "Masugu" culture of direct and honest integrity, creates a service environment where communication is seamless, and every interaction is handled with a level of professional gravity that has become the hallmark of the Sanese national carrier.

==Fleet==
Okaiken Air maintains one of the most modern and fuel-efficient fleets in the East Fosian Sphere. The airline primarily utilizes Boeing aircraft for its long-haul operations while favoring Airbus for regional and domestic trunk routes.

